\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Every ratio $r$ below is the paired width ratio of \cref{sec:experiments}; ``label saving'' is $1-r^2$. Coverage is nominal 95\%, from at least 2000 repetitions per cell.

\paragraph{Real data: PPI++ is within about 3\% of the best interval we can build.}
At each dataset's reference $n$ the selected cross-fitted interval has $r$ between $0.971$ and $1.005$ against PPI++ on the nine datasets (\cref{fig:ladder}, \cref{tab:main}). Eight are classified \emph{no gain}; the exception is \texttt{gene\_expression} ($r=0.971$, label saving $5.7\%$), which falls in the undecided band, and \texttt{census\_income} ($r=0.992$, saving $1.6\%$) is the next largest. The estimated floor has a predicted ratio within $[0.993,1.005]$ on every dataset, so no interval built from the same labels, unlabeled records and $f$ in the model classes we fit is more than about 1\% shorter than PPI++ at that~$n$. Across the whole grid of $51$ dataset--$n$ cells ($n$ from 30 to 3000), $39$ are no gain, $12$ undecided and one is a gain: \texttt{gene\_expression} at $n=100$ ($r=0.930$, saving $13\%$). The cells that fall in the undecided band are mostly at the smallest $n$, where learning a calibrator makes the interval \emph{wider} than PPI++ (at $n=30$, $r$ is up to $1.116$ on \texttt{plankton}). The labels-only interval is $1.15$ to $2.9$ times PPI++ (\cref{fig:ladder}), so the free step from PPI to PPI++ and the use of $f$ matter far more than anything that remains.

\paragraph{Where there is room, and what it costs to take it.}
The same estimator gains a lot when $\E[Y\mid f]$ is far from affine. In the synthetic design $Y=0.8f+c(f^2-1)+0.6\varepsilon$ with $f\sim N(0,1)$ (so $\Delta=2c^2$), $r$ at $n=1600$ is $0.73$ for $c=0.5$ (saving $47\%$), $0.93$ for $c=0.2$ and $0.998$ for $c=0.05$; with a saturating $\tanh$ calibration it is $0.85$, with a non-monotone $\sin$ calibration $0.81$, and with an exactly affine $\E[Y\mid f]$ it is $1.001$, as \cref{cor:noroom} requires. \Cref{fig:breakeven} shows the price. At small $n$ the calibrated arms are wider than PPI++ (up to $r=1.21$ at $n=25$ for isotonic calibration with $c=0.05$), and cross below $r=1$ near the predicted break-even $n^\ast$. The formula is a heuristic, and it is right only to a point. On the synthetic quadratic designs the measured first winning grid sizes bracket it within the factor of three we fixed beforehand; the predicted $n^\ast$ was $6.3$, $40$, $158$ and $632$ for $c=0.5,0.2,0.1,0.05$, and the first grid $n$ at which the arm wins was $25$ (the smallest tried), $100$, $800$, $800$. For the real datasets the prediction is above the largest $n$ we ran on eight of nine (for example $2\times10^4$ for \texttt{galaxies}, where $\Delta$ is $3\times10^{-5}$) and no win is seen there, which agrees. It fails on \texttt{gene\_expression}, where $n^\ast$ is predicted as $355$ while the selected arm already wins by $n=100$, and on one transformed-predictor stress case. We attribute neither miss to a tested cause here.

\paragraph{The closed form for the excess.}
The excess variance of PPI++ over the floor, $(1/n-1/M)\Delta$ (\cref{eq:gap}), matches the Monte Carlo value on the synthetic populations (right panel of \cref{fig:breakeven}): in the paired comparison with the oracle using the true $\E[Y\mid f]$, the measured-to-predicted ratio is near one wherever the prediction is not negligible, and the affine design has no measurable excess. Estimators that learn a calibrator pay an additional learning cost, which the closed form separates but does not remove.

